===persian===

در ماسه های چشمه خشکیده خیال
 چون ریشه مانده ای و نمی رویی از زمین
 شاخی نمی کنی که دمی غنچه امید
 در قلب دور مانده از شادی م وا شود
 برگی نمی کنی که دمی سایه ای کند
 این دل از تیرهای جگرسوز آفتاب
 یک دم رها شود.

این موی ریشه های تو
چون مار زخم دار
دیوانه وار میدود و می دواندم
در شوره زار داغ تنم تاب می خورد
آنگه به سوی ساغر می میکشاندم
آنقدر میخورم کز هوش میروم

ای ساقه امید
مردم در انتظار نیش خدنگ جوانه ات
از خاک شعله برکش و چون سالهای پیش
سر کن ترانه ات
ای اشکهای کوچک پرشادی بهار
من بغض خاموشم مردم در انتظار

بروجرد ۱۳۳۷

===machine===

In the sands of the dried spring, the imagination is like a root and does not grow. You do not make a branch from the ground that will eventually blossom. A bud of hope in the heart that is far from happiness. You do not make a leaf that will eventually create a shadow. This heart is free from the burning rays of the sun.

This hair of your roots
Like a wounded snake
I would run and run like crazy
My body sways in the hot salt marsh
Then I would pull it towards the ocean
I would eat so much that I would lose consciousness

O stem of hope
People are waiting for the sting of the mongoose of your bud
Rise a flame from the soil and, like in the past years
Sing your song
O small tears of spring joy
I am a silent grudge, people are waiting

Borojerd 1337

===lantern===

In the sands of imagination's dried-up spring
you have stayed like a root and will not grow from the earth.
You put out no branch on which, for an instant, a bud of hope
might open in this heart kept far from joy;
you put out no leaf to cast a moment's shade,
so that this heart, from the liver-scorching arrows of the sun,
might for one breath go free.

This hair of your roots,
like a wounded snake,
runs wild and sets me running,
writhing in the hot salt-flat of my body,
then drags me toward the wine-cup —
and I drink until I lose my senses.

O stalk of hope:
I died waiting for the arrow-sting of your sprouting.
Draw up a flame from the soil and, as in years gone by,
strike up your song.
O little tears, brimming with the joy of spring —
I am the silent sob; I died, waiting.

Borujerd, 1337

